## Actual Behavior

TODO: Describe actual behavior

Reported 2026-08-31 by the tm-tournament-station session, which hit it while we
were probing concurrently. **Our requests are in their log, interleaved in the
same second, both times it happened. We were the other client.**

## Evidence (their log, verbatim)

    Read payload: {"route": "load_plugin", "data": {"id": "tournament-station", "s
    Bytes to read: 34
    Read payload: ource": "user", "type": "folder"}}

One request split across two socket reads; the game-side plugin parsed **each
half as a complete payload**. The second fragment is invalid JSON, the route
came out empty, and the call failed with `Not implemented:` (empty route).

## Why this is a probe-integrity ticket and not someone else's tooling bug

Every game verdict this repo relies on — B052/B053 corpora, B068's re-probe,
B071's ten-case table, B080's provenance pins, every issue repro — travels over
this transport.

This is a **second contamination channel**, independent of the one B068 and
B077 closed, and none of those guards watch it:

| channel | guard | what it damages |
|---|---|---|
| batched compilation unit | B068 (one name per unit) | an abort marks the rest ACCEPTED |
| stranded fixture in Plugins/ | B077 (registration-before-copy, fail-closed sweep) | an ACCEPT reads a leftover plugin |
| **split payload on the wire** | **NONE** | **the game answers a question nobody asked** |

**The third is the worst of them, for one specific reason.** A contaminated
fixture makes an ACCEPT untrustworthy but leaves the transaction intact — you
asked X and got an answer about X in a dirty room. A mangled payload can make
the game compile something that is not what was sent, and **a truncated script
is still a script — it can compile.** So the corrupted case can return a clean,
plausible, WRONG verdict with nothing in the output marking it as damaged.
Every existing guard assumes the question arrived intact.

## Likely root cause, and the check that would confirm it

`Bytes to read: 34` followed by a fragment beginning `ource":` looks like the
reader treating each `read()` return as a complete message instead of
accumulating to the declared length. If so, **concurrency is not the cause, only
the trigger** — it makes splitting likely, but any payload large enough to split
would corrupt with a single client.

That is a falsifiable prediction and it is worth one test: send a single large
payload from ONE client with nothing else on the port. If it corrupts, this is
"the reader does not honour its own length prefix", which is a much easier bug
to fix and a much stronger report than "concurrent clients interfere".

Predicted, so state it before measuring: if the single-client large-payload test
passes cleanly, the length-prefix theory is WRONG and it really is a
concurrency/interleaving bug in the read loop. Do not retro-fit the explanation
to whichever result appears.

## Consequences to act on

- **`Error receiving header bytes` was written off as flakiness by two sessions
  today.** That write-off now looks wrong; it is the same family. Stop treating
  it as noise.
- Probe verdicts require **confirmed-exclusive port access**, and the tooling
  should say so and ideally check it, rather than relying on a coordinator
  serialising lanes by hand — which is what was in force today and did not
  prevent this, because the other client was a different session entirely.
- Provenance sidecars (B080) must record that a verdict was taken under
  exclusive access. A row measured beside another client is environment-
  dependent in exactly that ticket's sense, and the corpus cannot currently
  express it.
- Consider whether the probe tooling can DETECT the condition rather than merely
  avoid it — e.g. echo a nonce through the round trip and refuse any verdict
  whose response does not carry it back. A guard that detects beats a
  convention that coordinates, because conventions do not bind other sessions.

## Already done

lane-resolver's six-name B080 probe was halted mid-run and its results
DISCARDED rather than reconciled against a re-run: a verdict indistinguishable
from a corrupted verdict is not evidence, and "the two runs agreed" would
launder it rather than check it.

## Related

B068 (batched-unit channel), B077 (stranded-fixture channel), B080 (needs this
recorded as provenance), B092 (a different unexplained-result ticket from the
