## CORRECTION — THE ORIGINAL FRAMING OF THIS TICKET WAS WRONG (coordinator, 2026-08-31)

I filed this as "concurrent clients corrupt each other's payloads". **That is
not the mechanism.** Two lanes measured it independently and both refute it.
The title has been changed to match the measurement; the analysis below
supersedes the concurrency story above.

**Concurrency is not required. It is not even a factor.** The real defect:

- RemoteBuild's `HandleClient` calls `Json::Parse` on **each raw socket read**,
  rather than accumulating until a complete message has arrived.
- `Net::Socket::ReadRaw` returns at most ~64-66 bytes per call.
- So ANY request longer than that is split, and **every fragment fails to
  parse** — with a single client, on an idle port.

**Threshold measured, exact and deterministic**, 3 trials each with a padded
`get_status`: **66 bytes OK, 67 bytes FAIL**, everything above FAIL.

**`load_plugin` is therefore unreachable at any id length.** Its shortest
possible payload — compact, one-character id —

    {"route":"load_plugin","data":{"id":"a","source":"user","type":"folder"}}

is **72-73 bytes**. There is no plugin id short enough. Every game-probe path
in this repo goes through it.

I had actually predicted this and written the discriminating test into the
original filing: "it should reproduce with a single client if the payload is
big enough... that would turn 'concurrent clients corrupt each other' into 'the
reader does not honour its own length prefix'." The test was run and the
size theory won. **The prediction was recorded before the measurement, which is
the only reason it counts for anything** — and the concurrency framing, which
both I and the reporting session found intuitive, was simply wrong.

## Why the failure is so expensive to diagnose

The reply is `{"error":"Not implemented: ","data":""}` — **the router's message
for an UNKNOWN ROUTE, with an EMPTY route name**, because neither fragment
parsed. So the error points the reader at route SPELLINGS when the problem is
SIZE. Two sessions independently lost time to that today.

**`get_status` is 32-35 bytes and works.** So `rb_alive()` cheerfully returns
Alive, every probe passes its liveness gate, and then fails on the route that
matters. A liveness check that uses a short payload cannot detect this class of
fault — it is a positive control that does not exercise the failing path.

## THE DANGEROUS PART — read this before "fixing" anything

**Today the harness fails CLOSED, and that is the only thing saving us.**
B077's teardown guard sees the unload fail, calls `holder.preserve()`, and marks
the run SKIP — so no verdict escapes.

**If someone fixes `unload_plugin` without fixing `load_plugin`, the harness
will start emitting confident ACCEPTs for plugins the game never compiled.**
That is Mechanism 1 from the hazards doc — the probe cannot see what it was
asked about — with the polarity that stops you looking, because everything
would appear to work.

**Whoever fixes this must fix `load_plugin` FIRST and verify against the game's
own log that the payload arrived in ONE read.** Not that the call returned
success. The log.

## Second, separate defect — `fleet_dogfood.rb_route`

It sleeps 0.2s, does a single `recv(65536)`, then `json.loads(raw[4:])`. So it
ACKNOWLEDGES a 4-byte length header and then ignores the length. Responses ARE
length-prefixed and can arrive as two frames, so the second frame's header lands
inside the JSON text and it raises `Extra data: line 1 column 40 (char 39)`.

Loud, so it never produced a wrong verdict — but it **masks the size defect
behind a parse error**, and cost at least one lane real time being misread as an
unimplemented route. A correct reader loops on the length prefix. One exists at
`scripts/probe_export_binding.py:rb_route` (lane-tooling) and was deliberately
NOT patched into the shared file mid-batch.

Note the asymmetry worth keeping: REQUESTS are unframed (adding a length prefix
breaks even short payloads — tested), while RESPONSES are length-prefixed. That
asymmetry is real, not a misreading.

## Probable cause of onset, and why the shipped corpora are still good

The committed b052/b053 corpora were produced when this worked, and today's
B068 re-probe of all 1334 ACCEPTED rows also completed successfully. **The
Openplanet install on this machine was updated on disk at 15:10 today**
(1.29.5 -> 1.29.14; established with the tm-tournament-station session while
diagnosing an unrelated `Net::SecureSocket` removal). So the probe harness
worked before that update and is broken after it.

That timing matters twice over: it means **the corpora and the B068 re-probe
predate the breakage and remain valid**, and it means the likely trigger is a
behaviour change in `Net::Socket::ReadRaw` or in RemoteBuild.op between those
builds, rather than anything in this repo's scripts.

## Fix, and who may make it

`HandleClient` must accumulate reads until `Json::Parse` succeeds (or a declared
length is satisfied). The user's fork is at
`~/src/openplanet/my-plugins/tm-remote-build`, same code as the installed `.op`.

**Coordinator ruling: an agent may implement this in the fork ON A BRANCH, with
the log-verified one-read check above. No agent installs it into the live game
or touches the installed `.op` — that is Max's call.**

